                 [video]

Blur PII in videos and screen recordings (scroll-aware, no name list needed).

positional arguments:
  video                 input video (omit only with --batch)

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  -o OUTPUT, --output OUTPUT
  --config CONFIG       YAML config profile (CLI flags override it)
  --allow-names ALLOW_NAMES
                        text file of provider/staff names to KEEP visible
  --extra-names EXTRA_NAMES
                        text file of names to always blur
  --engine {auto,paddle,onnx,tesseract}
  --device {auto,cpu,gpu}
                        PaddleOCR compute device (default: gpu if available)
  --encoder {auto,nvenc,qsv,x264}
                        video encoder: auto = NVENC then QSV (GPU) if
                        available, else libx264 (CPU)
  --no-ner              disable spaCy NER
  --heuristic-names {auto,on,off}
                        capitalized-pair fallback: auto = on when NER
                        unavailable
  --sample-interval SAMPLE_INTERVAL
                        seconds between time-based OCR samples (default 0.5)
  --scan-trigger SCAN_TRIGGER
                        also OCR after this many pixels of scroll (default 60)
  --pad PAD, --blur-buffer PAD
                        blur buffer: pixels of blur beyond the tightly-cropped
                        word/face (default 8)
  --no-vfr-fix          skip automatic CFR normalization of VFR input
  --out-quality {archival,balanced,share}
                        output file quality/size: archival = visually lossless
                        (largest, the default), balanced = ~1/3 the size,
                        share = ~1/8 — still clean, sized for messaging and
                        upload
  --codec {h264,hevc}   video codec for the output: h264 (default, plays
                        everywhere) or hevc (H.265, smaller files). HDR output
                        always uses 10-bit HEVC regardless.
  --hdr-output {match,sdr}
                        for HDR input: 'match' (default) keeps the output HDR
                        (10-bit HEVC, PQ/HLG preserved); 'sdr' tone-maps the
                        output to SDR BT.709. SDR input always renders SDR —
                        output matches the source.
  --tile {auto,on,off}  tiled detection for small objects on high-res frames
                        (auto: engages when the frame is >=2.6x the model
                        input)
  --face-heads          also cover TURNED heads: derive a head region from
                        every person detection (needs a person model) and
                        union it into the face category — hair and head shape
                        identify people too
  --dense-faces         run the face detector on EVERY frame (not just at scan
                        intervals) so fast-moving faces stay covered.
                        Restricted to face detection zones when zones are set,
                        which keeps it fast. Higher render time.
  --dense-face-stride DENSE_FACE_STRIDE
                        run the per-frame detectors (faces, plates, person,
                        QR, screens, all-text) on every Nth frame; skipped
                        frames are covered by interpolating each track between
                        samples. 1 = every frame (default). Prefer --scan-
                        speed.
  --scan-speed {thorough,balanced,fast,fastest}
                        speed/coverage preset. thorough (default) = every
                        frame, full resolution. balanced = every 2nd frame,
                        gaps interpolated (~1.9x faster, coverage ~unchanged).
                        fast = every 3rd frame (~2.6x; fast-moving faces and
                        faces on screen for under 3 frames can be missed).
                        fastest = every 3rd frame at half face-detection
                        resolution (~8x; misses many small/distant faces —
                        close-ups only). Never slower than explicit --dense-
                        face-stride/--detect-scale. Always review the output.
  --plate-model PLATE_MODEL
                        path to a YOLOv8 license-plate ONNX model. If omitted,
                        OpenScrub looks for models/plate_yolov8.onnx or the
                        $OPENSCRUB_PLATE_MODEL env var. Plate category is
                        inactive without a model.
  --face-shape {ellipse,rect}
                        mask shape for face redaction: ellipse hugs the face
                        (no blurred background corners); rect is the classic
                        box. Text regions are always rectangular.
  --face-model FACE_MODEL
                        path to an optional face-detection ONNX model
                        (CenterFace or SCRFD, auto-recognized; see
                        face_models.json). Falls back to
                        $OPENSCRUB_FACE_MODEL, then to the built-in YuNet —
                        face detection always works without a model file.
  --plate-threshold PLATE_THRESHOLD
                        license-plate detector confidence cutoff (0-1).
                        Default 0.35.
  --person-model PERSON_MODEL
                        path to a YOLO person-detection ONNX model (see
                        person_models.json). If omitted, OpenScrub looks for
                        models/person_yolov8.onnx or $OPENSCRUB_PERSON_MODEL.
                        The person (full-body blur) category is inactive
                        without a model.
  --person-threshold PERSON_THRESHOLD
                        person detector confidence cutoff (0-1). Default 0.5.
  --face-threshold FACE_THRESHOLD
                        face detector confidence cutoff (0-1). Lower catches
                        more faces but risks false positives; higher is
                        stricter. Default 0.6 (YuNet).
  --draw-scores         with --preview, draw each face's confidence score so
                        you can tune --face-threshold on your own footage
  --detect-scale DETECT_SCALE
                        run FACE detection on a downscaled copy of each frame
                        for speed (0.2-1.0; e.g. 0.5 = half resolution).
                        Output quality is unaffected. Default 1.0 (off).
  --face-expand FACE_EXPAND
                        expand detected face boxes by this fraction before the
                        blur buffer is applied (default 0.15). Close-up faces
                        get more automatically: the margin ramps up to 0.45 as
                        a face fills 15-30% of the frame (never below this
                        value), so the neck and collar under a close-up are
                        covered too
  --coverage {tight,box,concealed}
                        person/tracked-object cover: tight = silhouette (best
                        looking), box = full detection box (hides body shape),
                        concealed = gliding oversized box (witness-grade: also
                        hides walking gait)
  --mode {blur,box,mosaic,inpaint}
                        default redaction style: blur (reversible-ish), box
                        (solid black, irreversible), or mosaic (pixelation —
                        censored look, not recoverable)
  --mode-map MODE_MAP   per-category overrides, e.g. 'ssn=box,mrn=box' —
                        override style per category, e.g.
                        'ssn=box,face=mosaic'; a risky while blurring the
                        rest. Categories not listed use --mode.
  --mrn-regex MRN_REGEX
                        your own pattern for the 'mrn' ID-number category
                        (record/claim/account numbers — any identifier
                        format). EMPTY (the default) leaves the category
                        inactive: bring your own pattern, e.g. '\b\d{7}\b' or
                        '^[A-Za-z]{0,3}\d{6,10}$'.
  --scroll-track {auto,on,off}
                        screen-scroll tracking + safety bands. 'auto'
                        (default) probes the footage: camera video (continuous
                        2-axis motion) disables them — detections stay screen-
                        anchored and no unscanned-strip bands are drawn.
                        'on'/'off' force it.
  --adaptive {on,off}   self-tune scan pacing: stretch the sample interval
                        while the screen is static, tighten it under heavy
                        change, and scan sooner when scrolling fast. 'off'
                        uses the fixed values.
  --phrase TEXT         text to find and blur for the 'phrase' (specific text)
                        category, repeatable: --categories phrase --phrase
                        "Acme Corp". Case, spacing and punctuation are
                        ignored; 4+ characters also match inside longer words
  --phrases FILE        text file of phrases for the 'phrase' category, one
                        per line (same matching as --phrase)
  --custom-regex ID=PATTERN
                        user-defined regex category (repeatable), e.g.
                        --custom-regex claim=CLM-\d+ . Matches are detections
                        in category ID: they appear in review, reports, per-
                        category modes, and detection zones like any built-in
                        category. Add ID to --categories to enable it.
  --bridge-gap BRIDGE_GAP
                        max seconds to bridge blur across OCR misses when the
                        same PII reappears in the same region (default 4.0)
  --no-memory           disable PII text memory (recall of previously
                        confirmed strings)
  --preview             draw boxes (red=PII, orange=unscanned band) instead of
                        blurring
  --report REPORT       write JSON audit report with provenance (contains PII
                        text — protect it)
  --detect-windows DETECT_WINDOWS
                        scan ONLY these time ranges, e.g. '60-90,300-330' —
                        multiple detection windows; the video between them is
                        skipped entirely when possible. Empty = whole video
                        (minus --skip-start/--skip-end).
  --detect-windows-frac DETECT_WINDOWS_FRAC
                        like --detect-windows but FRACTIONS of the video
                        duration (0-1), e.g. '0.25-0.4,0.8-0.9'. The web UI
                        sends these so the browser's reported length can never
                        desync from the server's measurement (iPhone HEVC/VFR
                        reported different durations). Overrides --detect-
                        windows when set.
  --clip-frac CLIP_FRAC
                        output trim as FRACTIONS of the duration: 'f0-f1'
                        (0-1). Overrides --clip-start/--clip-end. Same
                        duration-agnostic reason as --detect-windows-frac.
  --mute-audio-tracks MUTE_AUDIO_TRACKS
                        audio tracks to remove from the output: comma list of
                        1-based track numbers, or 'all'. Multi-track sources
                        (game + mic, camera + lav) keep their other tracks.
                        Numbers count every track unless --audio-lane-count
                        says otherwise.
  --audio-lane-count AUDIO_LANE_COUNT
                        how many audio lanes the web editor showed (set by the
                        web UI). Browsers list only playable tracks, so
                        --mute-audio-tracks numbers are matched against all
                        tracks or the decodable ones, whichever this count
                        fits; an ambiguous match removes ALL audio (fail
                        closed).
  --clip-start CLIP_START
                        output trim: drop everything before this second — the
                        redacted output starts here
  --clip-end CLIP_END   output trim: drop everything after this second (0 =
                        keep to the end)
  --audio-pii           transcribe the audio LOCALLY (faster-whisper) and
                        suggest mute spans for spoken PII in the selected
                        categories — suggestions appear in review and the
                        report; nothing leaves the machine
  --audio-pii-model AUDIO_PII_MODEL
                        whisper model size for --audio-pii (tiny/base/small;
                        larger = slower + better)
  --audio-pii-apply     apply the spoken-PII suggestions as MUTE spans
                        automatically instead of waiting for review
  --audio-redact AUDIO_REDACT
                        time spans to silence in the OUTPUT's audio track,
                        e.g. '12.5-19.0,84-90'. Spoken names and numbers leak
                        PII too. See --audio-redact-mode.
  --audio-redact-mode {mute,bleep}
                        mute = silence the spans; bleep = 1 kHz tone over them
                        (audible edit instead of a suspicious gap)
  --from-report FROM_REPORT
                        skip scanning; re-render from an (edited) audit report
                        produced by --report
  --batch BATCH         process every video in this folder; files whose output
                        already exists are skipped (resume)
  --overwrite           with --batch: reprocess even if output exists
  --backtrack-window BACKTRACK_WINDOW
                        seconds of recent frames kept for onset backtracking
                        (RAM: ~1MB per frame at 1440p; default 2.5)
  --no-backtrack        disable onset backtracking (finding the exact frame
                        where newly detected PII first appeared)
  --skip-start SKIP_START
                        don't detect anything during the first N seconds
  --skip-end SKIP_END   stop detecting N seconds before the end of the video
  --zones ZONES         JSON file of per-category detection zones (normalized
                        0-1 coords). Categories with zones are ONLY detected
                        inside them — detections outside are dropped and
                        counted as a warning. Categories without zones remain
                        full-frame.
  --windows WINDOWS     JSON file describing per-time-window detection scope
                        from the unified editor: a list of {"t0","t1"
                        (FRACTIONS 0-1 of duration), "zones": {cat:
                        [normrects]}}. Each window scans ONLY its time range,
                        and ONLY inside its own zones (a window with no zones
                        scans its whole frame). Supersedes --zones / --detect-
                        windows when present. The whole-clip default is one
                        window covering the video.
  --ignore-region X1,Y1,X2,Y2
                        screen region to never blur (repeatable), e.g. taskbar
                        clock
  --ocr-upscale {auto,on,off}
                        re-OCR at 2x when text is small (default auto)
  --paranoid            maximum-recall preset: dense sampling, lenient
                        matching, forced upscale — more false positives, clean
                        them up in review
  --vfr {auto,ignore}   auto: detect variable frame rate and normalize to CFR
                        before processing (default); ignore: skip check
  --categories CATEGORIES
